In this lesson, we'll look at how to find the maximum or minimum value of something in a real‑world style problem. The first step is to build a formula for the thing you want to make as big or as small as possible, and write it using just one variable. Next, you need to figure out the domain, meaning what values that variable is actually allowed to take, since often there are physical or practical limits. Once you've got the formula and the domain, differentiate it and set the derivative equal to zero. That gives you the stationary point. Now here's the key bit: the global maximum or minimum might not be at the stationary point at all. It could be at one of the endpoints of your domain. So always check the stationary point AND the endpoints, then compare their values to decide which is the true max or min. And a quick justification tip: if you've only got one stationary point in the middle and the function heads off to plus or minus infinity at both ends, that single stationary point has to be your global extremum. Now let's see it in action.